WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Also there's some things that are just

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.881
intrinsic to buildings that make it to

00:00:03.881 --> 00:00:05.921
where automating and connecting

00:00:05.921 --> 00:00:07.541
everything don't work. Like one of the

00:00:07.541 --> 00:00:09.220
big things is: why can't we make buildings

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.751
the way Tesla makes cars?

00:00:10.751 --> 00:00:13.631
Tesla automated the heck out of making cars.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.840
And you have robots do all this assembly.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.820
It's absolutely beautiful. They automated the line.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.691
So that it can make this car today

00:00:19.691 --> 00:00:22.329
and make a different type of car tomorrow.

00:00:22.329 --> 00:00:23.711
It's really impressive.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.320
Well one of the big reasons why

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.198
we can't make buildings the way they make cars is:

00:00:28.198 --> 00:00:29.913
Cars are a mass-produced item

00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:32.777
that's made entirely from custom parts, right?

00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:35.408
The doors for a Tesla don't fit on my Subaru.

00:00:35.408 --> 00:00:37.030
They don't fit on my Prius.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.191
The doors for a Tesla are just for a Tesla.

00:00:39.191 --> 00:00:40.822
They're all custom-made parts

00:00:40.822 --> 00:00:42.400
for a mass produced item.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.122
Buildings are the opposite.

00:00:44.122 --> 00:00:47.900
It's a totally custom item that's made
almost entirely from mass-produced parts.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.011
All those pieces that's just timber that

00:00:51.011 --> 00:00:52.900
you can buy from a lumberyard.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.100
Those straps and stuff are off-the-shelf items

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.040
or they were just welded up at a shop somewhere.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.880
Buildings are 90% just off-the-shelf stuff.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.920
That's minimally modified

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.760
if modified at all.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.960
And put together into a totally custom one-off thing.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.040
Part of that is because of this.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.751
Buildings are a result of a lot of different

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:15.730
forces coming together at the same time.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.757
So you have cultural things that are happening,

00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:22.300
where a building that looks like a hospital here in the

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.791
Bay Area would not fly in Philadelphia.

00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:28.151
Philadelphia expects a hospital to look different.

00:01:28.151 --> 00:01:31.201
Just culturally. Local culture is a big issue.

00:01:31.201 --> 00:01:33.190
There's also political issues.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.140
Like trying to build on the San Francisco waterfront

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.471
or trying to build in Oakland or different areas

00:01:38.471 --> 00:01:41.092
there's local codes and local government things

00:01:41.092 --> 00:01:42.282
you have to deal with.

00:01:42.282 --> 00:01:45.041
Economics impacts areas like what's viable to build

00:01:45.041 --> 00:01:48.311
here in California would cost

00:01:48.311 --> 00:01:50.351
twice as much to build somewhere else

00:01:50.351 --> 00:01:51.900
or take twice as much to heat.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.980
With a performance issue. Like if you build

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.021
a typical California house and you put it

00:01:56.021 --> 00:01:58.420
in the Arizona desert, like they did in Phoenix.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.560
Now you're paying hundreds of dollars

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.040
in cooling bills every summer because you had

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.420
your giant picture window that looked out

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.340
at your swimming pool.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.080
Which is a terrible idea to have in Arizona.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.412
But hey, you know.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.700
So every building is a weird combination

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.260
of hyperlocal issues, right?

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.220
And so it's almost impossible to think

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.120
that we could crank out mass-produced buildings

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.318
that would fit. Because everybody would want it

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.380
customized and edited for their specific spot.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.307
Also the construction industry kind of

00:02:26.307 --> 00:02:27.860
works at its own time scale.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.665
This is from Stewart Brand's talk that was here

00:02:29.665 --> 00:02:30.740
just a little while ago.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.820
Who was here at that talk? it was great.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.160
So this idea of "Pace Layers."

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.040
These layers move at different speeds, right?

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.500
So we're mostly in that infrastructure layer.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.900
The building industry is the last

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.540
to adopt new technology and that's

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.260
not always because they're stingy

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.240
or because they're scared of new technology.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.400
Not to call you out, Dylan, but...

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.980
(my architect friend here): how long did you work

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.920
on the Stanford project you worked on?

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.260
But how long did the project go for?

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.640
Like 4 or 5 years.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.720
Think of how much technology has changed

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.860
in five years when it comes

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.140
to these things [mobile phone], right?

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.380
There's hospital projects that are

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.960
15 or 20 years in the making.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.740
By the time the project is done

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.560
whatever CAD system you were using for the project

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.020
won't run on modern operating systems anymore.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.688
And yet you're contractually obliged

00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:23.666
by the contract to deliver the CAD files in that format.

00:03:23.666 --> 00:03:25.710
So you're running virtualized systems

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.414
that are running old OSs' to be able

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.160
to run old CAD systems to be able to... yeah.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.200
Yeah. It gets ugly fast.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.092
But also really big contractors, really big builders,

00:03:35.092 --> 00:03:36.493
really big developers,

00:03:36.493 --> 00:03:39.931
they're really just risk and resource managers.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.660
The really big contractors that are building

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.240
those hospitals or building the skyscrapers

00:03:44.243 --> 00:03:45.792
in downtown San Francisco

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.200
they might not have a single person on staff

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.330
that actually does physical construction.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.820
They're almost finance companies at that point--

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:55.780
and they're literally...

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:56.923
some of the larger contractors

00:03:56.923 --> 00:03:57.990
have people working for them

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.147
that are almost like hedge fund managers

00:04:00.147 --> 00:04:01.710
that are buying steel futures.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.040
So seven years from now when you buy the steel

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.220
to build your hospital it's hedged

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.640
in case steel doubles in price in that seven years.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.480
So they're very risk-averse.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.520
So unless they know that a technology is going to work

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.060
they don't really go into it.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.040
But then also, weirdly enough,

00:04:16.045 --> 00:04:19.673
part of the building industry works at that top layer, too.

00:04:19.673 --> 00:04:21.737
Where there's last minute changes

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.840
for a variety of reasons.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.120
Planning around certain things can be really difficult.

00:04:27.122 --> 00:04:28.905
Like hospital projects have to--

00:04:28.905 --> 00:04:31.580
most hospital projects, because they take

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.180
five to seven years to build

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.820
by the time the project is done

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.480
the MRI machines that they planned

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.000
to have in the hospital are totally outdated now.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.500
So they purposely planned... okay, 7 years from now

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.980
when they actually buy the real machine

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.060
it has to fit in the room

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.120
that we designed seven years ago.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.180
So there's all this crazy stuff that happened.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.400
So the timescale, too, is something that

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.456
lends itself to where it's very difficult

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:55.060
for us to mass-produce buildings

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.600
in the way we build cars.

